From 73a5b20b7f8f4ebfcda6a26cd576ad48c9499fac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 22:37:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tests): retain dead-port custody through the proxy
 hold-then-splice test [daveey]

test_proxy_holds_a_client_while_no_backend_answers_then_splices_it published
socket_free_port(), a bind-and-close port. A foreign listener could take it
before Proxy._upstream dialled, so the held client was spliced to that peer
instead of waiting for the roller's switch: the b"pong:ping" check failed or
reader.read timed out. Same mechanism PR #3329 fixed in the sibling hold/EOF
test (review t_84067f39a92b8150 follow-up).

Keep a non-listening socket bound to the dead port for the whole scenario,
reusing #3329's custody and its backend_port_contender fixture (now one-shot:
it contends only for the first published port, the dead one, so the later
switch to the live backend does not re-bind its already-bound socket). The
now-unused socket_free_port helper is removed.

RED: with the contender fixture applied and the test body unchanged
(socket_free_port), [port-takeover] fails with TimeoutError at the 5 s
reader.read while [uncontended] passes. GREEN: whole file 32 passed (plain
pytest and hermes-core-wt test); proxy subset 7 passed x5. Hold (hold_s=10,
0.6 s), 5 s read timeout, assertions and production code unchanged; no new
sleeps or retry changes.

Task: t_711efc96116c2c0b
